#| tag::doc[]

[[b2.reference.tools.qemu]]
= QEMU

The `qemu` module runs the tests of `run`, `unit-test` and the other testing
rules under https://www.qemu.org[QEMU] when the target is for a processor the
host can not run natively:

* the tests of Linux targets under QEMU user mode emulation, on Linux, or
  through a command that runs QEMU in Linux, such as a container, on other
  hosts,
* and the tests of bare metal targets under QEMU system emulation, with
  semihosting, on any host but Windows.

It is enabled by configuring it:

[source]
----
using qemu ;
----

With that, for example:

[source,shell]
----
b2 toolset=gcc-riscv architecture=riscv address-model=64
----

runs the tests as `qemu-riscv64 -L /usr/riscv64-linux-gnu bin/.../test`. The
emulator is chosen from `<architecture>` and `<address-model>`, and the QEMU
CPU from `<instruction-set>`. A test with an explicit `<testing.launcher>` is
run with that instead.

The syntax is:

[source]
----
using qemu : [version] : [command] : [options] ;
----

where:

`version`::
  The version of QEMU. If QEMU is found, it must be this version. If not
  given, it is the version of the QEMU found, and there must be one.
`command`::
  The command that runs one of the QEMU programs, such as
  `/opt/qemu/bin/qemu-aarch64`. The other QEMU programs are in the same
  directory. Any words before the program are a command that the QEMU user
  mode programs are run with, such as one that runs them in a container. If
  not given, the QEMU programs are searched for in `PATH`.

and the `options` are any of:

`<qemu-emulator>ARCHITECTURE.ADDRESS-MODEL=PROGRAM`::
  The QEMU program to use for an `architecture` and `address-model`, in place
  of the default one, for example `<qemu-emulator>mips.32=qemu-mips` for a
  big endian target.
`<qemu-cpu>INSTRUCTION-SET=[CPU]`::
  The QEMU `-cpu` to use for an `instruction-set`, for example
  `<qemu-cpu>cortex-r5+vfpv3-d16=cortex-r5f`. Without a `CPU` no `-cpu` is
  given, for machines that only have their own.
`<qemu-sysroot>[ARCHITECTURE.ADDRESS-MODEL=]DIRECTORY`::
  The target system root that QEMU loads the dynamic linker and shared
  libraries from, passed as `-L`. Without an `ARCHITECTURE.ADDRESS-MODEL=` it
  applies to all targets. If not given, and `QEMU_LD_PREFIX` is not set, the
  Debian style `/usr/<triplet>` of the target is used when it exists.
`<qemu-option>OPTION`::
  An additional option for all QEMU runs, for example `<qemu-option>-B` and
  `<qemu-option>0x100000`.
`<qemu-machine>[KEY=]MACHINE`::
  The QEMU machine, `-M`, to run bare metal tests on. The `KEY` is an
  `instruction-set`, or an `ARCHITECTURE.ADDRESS-MODEL`, that the machine is
  for, and without one it is for all bare metal targets. For example
  `<qemu-machine>cortex-a9+vfpv3=realview-pbx-a9`. See below.
`<qemu-system>ARCHITECTURE.ADDRESS-MODEL=PROGRAM`::
  The QEMU system emulator to use for bare metal tests of an `architecture`
  and `address-model`, in place of the default one, for example
  `<qemu-system>mips.32=qemu-system-mips` for a big endian target.
`<qemu-check>on|off`::
  With `on`, runs each QEMU program that tests would be run with, and warns
  if it is not found, is not the version of QEMU, or does not list the CPU of
  an `instruction-set`. Normally the QEMU programs and CPUs are not checked,
  but trusted to be right, and with `--debug-configuration` the CPUs each
  program lists are printed.

The QEMU `-cpu` for an `instruction-set` is the one given with
`<qemu-cpu>`, else the QEMU name from a built in table of names that differ
between B2 and QEMU, else the `instruction-set` value itself. QEMU is not run
to check it, nor that the emulator is installed, so a test with a CPU the
emulator does not have fails with QEMU's error. Each `architecture` is
configured for the `instruction-set` values that B2 groups with it, and for
any in no group. Tests for an `architecture` without an `address-model`, or
for a target with no known emulator, fail saying so.

B2 has no feature for the byte order of the target, so the targets are run
with the little endian emulators, such as `qemu-mipsel` and `qemu-ppc64le`.
Big endian targets need a `<qemu-emulator>`. The `instruction-set` values are
the ones known when `using qemu` is called, so it must be called after any
extension of the `instruction-set` feature.

QEMU user mode emulation is only available on Linux hosts. On other hosts the
tests of Linux targets can be run in Linux with a command, which B2 puts in
front of the QEMU program and the test. For example, on macOS, with a
container of the `container` tool that has QEMU installed, and the build
directory mounted at the same path, as the tests are run in the directory B2
is run in:

[source,shell]
----
container run -d --name b2-qemu -v "$HOME:$HOME" debian:stable sleep infinity
container exec b2-qemu sh -c \
    "apt-get update && apt-get install -y qemu-user libc6-riscv64-cross"
----

and:

[source]
----
using qemu : : container exec -w $PWD -e LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH
    b2-qemu qemu-riscv64 ;
----

The command passes on the library path that the test sets up. The tests of
Linux targets for the host's own processor are run with the command too, but
without QEMU. As the files QEMU sees can not be looked at, the Debian style
`/usr/<triplet>` system root is always passed to QEMU, which ignores it if
missing. On other hosts without such a command, `using qemu` warns, and the
tests of Linux targets are not run under QEMU.

The tests of bare metal targets, with `<target-os>elf` or `<target-os>none`,
are run with the `qemu-system-<arch>` emulator. They are always run on the
host, and with a command that has words before the QEMU program the
`qemu-system-<arch>` programs are searched for in `PATH`. There is no telling
which machine a bare metal target is for, so it must be given with
`<qemu-machine>`. The test is loaded as the kernel of the machine, and run
with semihosting, which passes it its arguments and its exit status, and
prints its output. For example, for a test linked with newlib's
`rdimon.specs`:

[source]
----
using qemu : : : <qemu-machine>cortex-a9+vfpv3=realview-pbx-a9 ;
----

runs it as `qemu-system-arm -M realview-pbx-a9 -cpu cortex-a9 -display none
-kernel test -semihosting-config enable=on,target=native,arg=test`, with
`-audio none` too for QEMU 8.2 and later. Output written to a serial port,
rather than with semihosting, needs a `<qemu-option>-serial` and
`<qemu-option>stdio`. A bare metal test that does not exit with semihosting
runs until it is stopped, which the `-l` option of B2 can limit.

|# # end::doc[]

import common ;
import errors ;
import feature ;
import modules ;
import numbers ;
import os ;
import path ;
import regex ;
import set ;
import testing ;
import toolset ;

if --debug-configuration in [ modules.peek : ARGV ]
{
    .debug-configuration = true ;
}

# The QEMU program for each <architecture>.<address-model> target. B2 does not
# know the byte order of the target, so these are the little endian ones where
# there is a choice. There is no little endian 32 bit PowerPC one.
.emulator.arm.64 = qemu-aarch64 ;
.emulator.arm.32 = qemu-arm ;
.emulator.riscv.64 = qemu-riscv64 ;
.emulator.riscv.32 = qemu-riscv32 ;
.emulator.loongarch.64 = qemu-loongarch64 ;
.emulator.s390x.64 = qemu-s390x ;
.emulator.sparc.64 = qemu-sparc64 ;
.emulator.sparc.32 = qemu-sparc ;
.emulator.parisc.32 = qemu-hppa ;
.emulator.x86.64 = qemu-x86_64 ;
.emulator.x86.32 = qemu-i386 ;
.emulator.power.64 = qemu-ppc64le ;
for local a in mips mips1 mips2 mips3 mips4 mips32 mips32r2 mips64
{
    .emulator.$(a).64 = qemu-mips64el ;
    .emulator.$(a).32 = qemu-mipsel ;
}

# The QEMU system emulator for each <architecture>.<address-model> bare metal
# target, little endian where there is a choice.
.system.arm.64 = qemu-system-aarch64 ;
.system.arm.32 = qemu-system-arm ;
.system.riscv.64 = qemu-system-riscv64 ;
.system.riscv.32 = qemu-system-riscv32 ;
.system.loongarch.64 = qemu-system-loongarch64 ;
.system.s390x.64 = qemu-system-s390x ;
.system.sparc.64 = qemu-system-sparc64 ;
.system.sparc.32 = qemu-system-sparc ;
.system.parisc.32 = qemu-system-hppa ;
.system.x86.64 = qemu-system-x86_64 ;
.system.x86.32 = qemu-system-i386 ;
.system.power.64 = qemu-system-ppc64 ;
.system.power.32 = qemu-system-ppc ;
for local a in mips mips1 mips2 mips3 mips4 mips32 mips32r2 mips64
{
    .system.$(a).64 = qemu-system-mips64el ;
    .system.$(a).32 = qemu-system-mipsel ;
}

# The Debian style triplets of the /usr/<triplet> system root for each QEMU
# program.
.sysroot.qemu-aarch64 = aarch64-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-aarch64_be = aarch64_be-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-arm = arm-linux-gnueabihf arm-linux-gnueabi ;
.sysroot.qemu-armeb = armeb-linux-gnueabihf armeb-linux-gnueabi ;
.sysroot.qemu-riscv64 = riscv64-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-riscv32 = riscv32-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-loongarch64 = loongarch64-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-s390x = s390x-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-sparc64 = sparc64-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-hppa = hppa-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-x86_64 = x86_64-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-i386 = i686-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-mips = mips-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-mipsel = mipsel-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-mips64 = mips64-linux-gnuabi64 ;
.sysroot.qemu-mips64el = mips64el-linux-gnuabi64 ;
.sysroot.qemu-ppc = powerpc-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-ppc64 = powerpc64-linux-gnu ;
.sysroot.qemu-ppc64le = powerpc64le-linux-gnu ;

# The QEMU CPU for <instruction-set> values whose name differs from QEMU's.
# Other values are passed to QEMU as they are.
.cpu.native = max ;
.cpu.cortex-a9+vfpv3 = cortex-a9 ;
.cpu.cortex-r5+vfpv3-d16 = cortex-r5f ;
.cpu.i486 = 486 ;
.cpu.core2 = core2duo ;
.cpu.conroe = Conroe ;
.cpu.penryn = Penryn ;
.cpu.nehalem = Nehalem ;
.cpu.sandy-bridge = SandyBridge ;
.cpu.ivy-bridge = IvyBridge ;
.cpu.haswell = Haswell ;
.cpu.broadwell = Broadwell ;
.cpu.skylake = Skylake-Client ;
.cpu.skylake-avx512 = Skylake-Server ;
.cpu.cascadelake = Cascadelake-Server ;
.cpu.cooperlake = Cooperlake ;
.cpu.icelake-server = Icelake-Server ;
.cpu.sapphirerapids = SapphireRapids ;
.cpu.atom = n270 ;
.cpu.znver1 = EPYC ;
.cpu.znver2 = EPYC-Rome ;
.cpu.znver3 = EPYC-Milan ;
.cpu.4kc = 4Kc ;
.cpu.4km = 4Km ;
.cpu.4kec = 4KEc ;
.cpu.4kem = 4KEm ;
.cpu.5kc = 5Kc ;
.cpu.5kf = 5Kf ;
.cpu.20kc = 20Kc ;
.cpu.24kc = 24Kc ;
.cpu.24kec = 24KEc ;
.cpu.24kf2_1 = 24Kf ;
.cpu.24kf1_1 = 24Kf ;
.cpu.34kf2_1 = 34Kf ;
.cpu.34kf1_1 = 34Kf ;
.cpu.74kf2_1 = 74Kf ;
.cpu.74kf1_1 = 74Kf ;
.cpu.74kf3_2 = 74Kf ;
.cpu.m14k = M14K ;
.cpu.m14kc = M14Kc ;
.cpu.p5600 = P5600 ;
.cpu.i6400 = I6400 ;
.cpu.i6500 = I6500 ;
.cpu.r4000 = R4000 ;
.cpu.loongson2e = Loongson-2E ;
.cpu.loongson2f = Loongson-2F ;
.cpu.loongson3a = Loongson-3A1000 ;

# The <instruction-set> values for each <architecture>, as B2 groups them in
# the instruction-set feature. An architecture is configured for its values,
# and for any in no group, such as ones added later.
.instruction-sets.x86 =
    native i486 i586 i686 pentium pentium-mmx pentiumpro pentium2 pentium3
    pentium3m pentium-m pentium4 pentium4m prescott nocona core2 corei7
    corei7-avx core-avx-i conroe conroe-xe conroe-l allendale merom merom-xe
    kentsfield kentsfield-xe penryn wolfdale yorksfield nehalem sandy-bridge
    ivy-bridge haswell broadwell skylake skylake-avx512 cannonlake
    icelake-client icelake-server cascadelake cooperlake tigerlake rocketlake
    alderlake sapphirerapids atom k6 k6-2 k6-3 athlon athlon-tbird athlon-4
    athlon-xp athlon-mp k8 opteron athlon64 athlon-fx k8-sse3 opteron-sse3
    athlon64-sse3 amdfam10 barcelona bdver1 bdver2 bdver3 bdver4 btver1 btver2
    znver1 znver2 znver3 winchip-c6 winchip2 c3 c3-2 c7 ;
.instruction-sets.ia64 = itanium itanium1 merced itanium2 mckinley ;
.instruction-sets.sparc =
    v7 cypress v8 supersparc sparclite hypersparc sparclite86x f930 f934
    sparclet tsc701 v9 ultrasparc ultrasparc3 ;
.instruction-sets.power =
    401 403 405 405fp 440 440fp 505 601 602 603 603e 604 604e 620 630 740 7400
    7450 750 801 821 823 860 970 8540 power-common ec603e g3 g4 g5 power power2
    power3 power4 power5 powerpc powerpc64 rios rios1 rsc rios2 rs64a ;
.instruction-sets.mips =
    4kc 4km 4kp 4ksc 4kec 4kem 4kep 4ksd 5kc 5kf 20kc 24kc 24kf2_1 24kf1_1
    24kec 24kef2_1 24kef1_1 34kc 34kf2_1 34kf1_1 34kn 74kc 74kf2_1 74kf1_1
    74kf3_2 1004kc 1004kf2_1 1004kf1_1 i6400 i6500 interaptiv loongson2e
    loongson2f loongson3a gs464 gs464e gs264e m4k m14k m14kc m14ke m14kec m5100
    m5101 octeon octeon+ octeon2 octeon3 orion p5600 p6600 r2000 r3000 r3900
    r4000 r4400 r4600 r4650 r4700 r5900 r6000 r8000 rm7000 rm9000 r10000 r12000
    r14000 r16000 sb1 sr71000 vr4100 vr4111 vr4120 vr4130 vr4300 vr5000 vr5400
    vr5500 xlr xlp ;
.instruction-sets.parisc = 700 7100 7100lc 7200 7300 8000 ;
.instruction-sets.arm =
    armv2 armv2a armv3 armv3m armv4 armv4t armv5 armv5t armv5te armv6 armv6j
    iwmmxt ep9312 armv7 armv7s cortex-a9+vfpv3 cortex-a53 cortex-r5
    cortex-r5+vfpv3-d16 ;
.instruction-sets.s390x = z196 zEC12 z13 z14 z15 ;
.instruction-set-groups = x86 ia64 sparc power mips parisc arm s390x ;

# The <architecture>.<address-model> targets that each host platform, as
# given by os.platform, runs natively. An empty address-model is the host's.
.native.X86 = x86.32 x86. ;
.native.X86_64 = x86.64 x86.32 x86. ;
.native.ARM = arm.32 arm. ;
.native.ARM64 = arm.64 arm. ;
.native.RISCV32 = riscv.32 riscv. ;
.native.RISCV64 = riscv.64 riscv. ;
.native.390 = s390x.64 s390x. ;
.native.PARISC = parisc.32 parisc. ;
.native.SPARC = sparc.32 sparc.64 sparc. ;
.native.MIPS32 = mips.32 mips. ;
.native.MIPS64 = mips.64 mips. ;
.native.PPC = power.32 power.64 power. ;
.native.IA64 = ia64.64 ia64. ;


# Configures running the tests of targets for other processors under QEMU.
#
rule init ( version ? : command * : options * )
{
    local arguments = $(version) ":" $(command) ":" $(options) ;
    arguments = $(arguments:J=" ") ;
    if $(.initialized)
    {
        if $(arguments) != $(.arguments)
        {
            ECHO "warning: qemu: already configured, ignoring using qemu :"
                "$(version:E=) : $(command:J= ) : $(options:J= ) ;" ;
        }
        return ;
    }
    .initialized = true ;
    .arguments = $(arguments) ;

    parse-options $(options) ;

    # The command is an optional command to run QEMU with, followed by one of
    # the QEMU programs, whose directory has the others.
    if $(command)
    {
        .wrapper = $(command[1--2]) ;
        .command-program = $(command[-1]:D=) ;
        .directory = $(command[-1]:D) ;
    }

    # The version must be known, and is that of the QEMU found.
    local found = [ find-version ] ;
    if $(version) && $(found) && ! [ version-matches $(version) : $(found) ]
    {
        errors.user-error "qemu: version $(version) was given, but QEMU"
            "version $(found) was found." ;
    }
    .version = $(found) $(version) ;
    .version = $(.version[1]) ;
    if ! $(.version)
    {
        errors.user-error "qemu: no QEMU was found to get its version from,"
            "give the version, or the command of QEMU, in using qemu." ;
    }
    .system-version = [ find-version system ] $(.version) ;
    .system-version = $(.system-version[1]) ;
    if $(.debug-configuration)
    {
        ECHO "notice: [qemu] version $(.version), system emulation version"
            "$(.system-version)" ;
    }

    # Without an audio backend, which QEMU 8.2 has, machines with audio
    # devices print warnings about the host's audio.
    if ! [ version-less $(.system-version) : 8.2 ]
    {
        .system-options = -audio none ;
    }

    # Bare metal targets run under system emulation, which needs a shell to
    # pass the test and its arguments to QEMU.
    if [ os.name ] != NT
    {
        configure-bare-metal ;
    }

    if [ os.name ] != LINUX && ! $(.wrapper)
    {
        ECHO "warning: qemu: user mode emulation is only available on Linux"
            "hosts, tests of Linux targets will not be run under QEMU. Give a"
            "command that runs QEMU in Linux in using qemu." ;
        return ;
    }

    local native = $(.native.$(.host-platform)) ;
    local address-models = [ feature.values <address-model> ] ;
    for local a in [ feature.values <architecture> ]
    {
        for local m in "" $(address-models)
        {
            if ! $(a).$(m) in $(native)
            {
                configure-target $(a) : $(m) ;
            }
            else if $(.wrapper)
            {
                # Native targets still need the command.
                local condition = <target-os>linux/<architecture>$(a)
                    /<address-model>$(m)/<testing.launcher> ;
                toolset.flags testing LAUNCHER $(condition:J=)
                    : "$(.wrapper:J= )" : unchecked ;
            }
        }
    }
    if $(.wrapper)
    {
        toolset.flags testing LAUNCHER
            <target-os>linux/<architecture>/<testing.launcher>
            : "$(.wrapper:J= )" : unchecked ;
    }
}
.host-platform = [ os.platform ] ;


# Checks and sets the options.
#
local rule parse-options ( options * )
{
    for local o in $(options)
    {
        local value = $(o:G=) ;
        local m = [ MATCH "^([^=]+)=(.*)$" : $(value) ] ;
        switch $(o:G)
        {
            case <qemu-emulator> :
                require $(o) : $(m[2]) ;
                .emulator.$(m[1]) = $(m[2]) ;
            case <qemu-system> :
                require $(o) : $(m[2]) ;
                .system.$(m[1]) = $(m[2]) ;
            case <qemu-cpu> :
                require $(o) : $(m[1]) ;
                if $(m[2]) { .user-cpu.$(m[1]) = $(m[2]) ; }
                else { .no-cpu.$(m[1]) = true ; }
            case <qemu-sysroot> :
                m = [ MATCH "^([^=/]+)=(.+)$" : $(value) ] ;
                if $(m) { .user-sysroot.$(m[1]) = $(m[2]) ; }
                else { .user-sysroot = $(value) ; }
            case <qemu-machine> :
                m = [ MATCH "^([^=,]+)=(.+)$" : $(value) ] ;
                if $(m) { .machine.$(m[1]) = $(m[2]) ; }
                else { .machine = $(value) ; }
                .machines = true ;
            case <qemu-option> :
                .qemu-options += $(value) ;
            case <qemu-check> :
                require $(o) : [ MATCH "^(on|off)$" : $(value) ] ;
                if $(value) = "on" { .check = true ; }
            case * :
                errors.user-error "qemu: unknown option $(o) in using qemu." ;
        }
    }
}


# Errors about the option unless it has the required value.
#
local rule require ( option : value * )
{
    if ! $(value)
    {
        errors.user-error "qemu: invalid option $(option) in using qemu." ;
    }
}


# Returns the <instruction-set> values to configure for the <architecture>.
#
local rule instruction-sets ( architecture )
{
    if ! $(.ungrouped-instruction-sets)
    {
        local all = [ feature.values <instruction-set> ] ;
        local grouped ;
        for local g in $(.instruction-set-groups)
        {
            .grouped-instruction-sets.$(g) =
                [ set.intersection $(.instruction-sets.$(g)) : $(all) ] ;
            grouped += $(.grouped-instruction-sets.$(g)) ;
        }
        .ungrouped-instruction-sets = [ set.difference $(all) : $(grouped) ]
            "" ;
    }
    local group = [ MATCH "^(mips)" : $(architecture) ] $(architecture) ;
    return $(.grouped-instruction-sets.$(group[1]))
        $(.ungrouped-instruction-sets[1--2]) ;
}


# Returns the version of QEMU, from the program of the command if given, else
# from the first user mode or system emulator found. With `system`, it is that
# of the first system emulator found on the host.
#
local rule find-version ( system ? )
{
    local output ;
    if $(.wrapper) && ! $(system)
    {
        local command = $(.wrapper) \"$(.command-program)\" --version ;
        output = [ SHELL "$(command:J= )" ] ;
    }
    else
    {
        local names = $(.command-program) ;
        if ! $(system) { names += [ programs .emulator ] ; }
        names += [ programs .system ] ;
        for local name in $(names)
        {
            if ! $(output)
            {
                local found ;
                if $(.directory) && ! $(.wrapper)
                {
                    found = [ path.glob [ path.make $(.directory) ] : $(name)
                        $(name).exe ] ;
                    if $(found) { found = [ path.native $(found[1]) ] ; }
                }
                else
                {
                    found = [ common.find-tool $(name) ] ;
                }
                if $(found)
                {
                    output = [ SHELL "\"$(found)\" --version" ] ;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return [ MATCH "version ([0-9]+[.][0-9.]*[0-9])" : $(output) ] ;
}


# Returns the QEMU programs of the table, .emulator or .system, once each.
#
local rule programs ( table )
{
    local result ;
    for local a in [ feature.values <architecture> ]
    {
        for local m in [ feature.values <address-model> ]
        {
            if ! $($(table).$(a).$(m)) in $(result)
            {
                result += $($(table).$(a).$(m)) ;
            }
        }
    }
    return $(result) ;
}


# Returns true if the given version, such as 10 or 10.0, is the found version,
# such as 10.0.13.
#
local rule version-matches ( given : found )
{
    local g = [ regex.split $(given) "[.]" ] ;
    local f = [ regex.split $(found) "[.]" ] ;
    while $(g) && $(g[1]) = $(f[1])
    {
        g = $(g[2-]) ;
        f = $(f[2-]) ;
    }
    if ! $(g) { return true ; }
}


# Returns true if the version is less than the other one.
#
local rule version-less ( version : other )
{
    local v = [ regex.split $(version) "[.]" ] ;
    local o = [ regex.split $(other) "[.]" ] ;
    while $(v) && $(o) && $(v[1]) = $(o[1])
    {
        v = $(v[2-]) ;
        o = $(o[2-]) ;
    }
    if $(o) && ( ! $(v) || [ numbers.less $(v[1]) $(o[1]) ] )
    {
        return true ;
    }
}


# Returns the QEMU program, quoted, in the directory of the command if given.
# With `host`, the program is run on the host, where the directory is only
# known if the command has no words before the QEMU program.
#
local rule program ( name : host ? )
{
    if $(.directory) && ! ( $(host) && $(.wrapper) )
    {
        name = [ path.native [ path.join [ path.make $(.directory) ]
            $(name) ] ] ;
    }
    return \"$(name)\" ;
}


# With --debug-configuration or <qemu-check>on, looks at the QEMU program and
# prints its CPUs, and with <qemu-check>on warns about its version and about
# CPUs it does not list.
#
local rule inspect ( command + : emulator : version : cpus * )
{
    if ! $(.debug-configuration) && ! $(.check)
    {
        return ;
    }
    if ! $(.inspected.$(emulator))
    {
        .inspected.$(emulator) = true ;
        local found = [ MATCH "version ([0-9]+[.][0-9.]*[0-9])"
            : [ SHELL "$(command:J= ) --version 2>&1" ] ] ;
        if ! $(found)
        {
            ECHO "warning: qemu: $(emulator) was not found." ;
            .missing.$(emulator) = true ;
            return ;
        }
        if $(.check) && $(found) != $(version)
        {
            ECHO "warning: qemu: $(emulator) is version $(found), not"
                "$(version)." ;
        }
        local listed ;
        for local line in [ SPLIT_BY_CHARACTERS
            [ SHELL "$(command:J= ) -cpu help 2>&1" ] : "\n" ]
        {
            if [ MATCH "(:)[ \t]*$" : $(line) ]
            {
                if $(listed) { break ; }
            }
            else
            {
                # QEMU 6 prefixes the CPUs with the architecture.
                line = [ MATCH "^[ \t]*(.*)" : $(line) ] ;
                line = [ MATCH "^(x86|s390|PowerPC|MIPS)[ \t]+(.*)" : $(line) ]
                    $(line) ;
                if $(line[3]) { line = $(line[2]) ; }
                listed += [ MATCH "^'?([^ \t']+)" : $(line[1]) ] ;
            }
        }
        .listed-cpus.$(emulator) = $(listed) ;
        ECHO "notice: [qemu] $(emulator) $(found) CPUs:" $(listed) ;
    }
    if $(.check) && ! $(.missing.$(emulator))
    {
        for local c in $(cpus)
        {
            if ! $(c) in $(.listed-cpus.$(emulator))
                && ! $(.warned.$(emulator).$(c))
            {
                .warned.$(emulator).$(c) = true ;
                ECHO "warning: qemu: $(emulator) does not list the CPU $(c)." ;
            }
        }
    }
}


# Returns the QEMU -cpu option for the <instruction-set> value, if any.
#
local rule cpu-option ( instruction-set ? )
{
    if $(instruction-set) && ! $(.no-cpu.$(instruction-set))
    {
        local cpu = $(.user-cpu.$(instruction-set)) $(.cpu.$(instruction-set))
            $(instruction-set) ;
        return -cpu $(cpu[1]) ;
    }
}


# Sets the launcher of the tests for one <architecture> and <address-model>.
#
local rule configure-target ( architecture : address-model ? )
{
    local target = $(architecture).$(address-model) ;
    local condition = <target-os>linux/<architecture>$(architecture)
        /<address-model>$(address-model)/<testing.launcher> ;
    condition = $(condition:J=) ;
    local emulator = $(.emulator.$(target)) ;

    if ! $(address-model)
    {
        if $(.emulator.$(architecture).64) || $(.emulator.$(architecture).32)
        {
            fail $(condition) : "architecture=$(architecture) needs an"
                "address-model to choose the QEMU emulator." ;
        }
        else
        {
            fail $(condition) : "no QEMU emulator is known for"
                "architecture=$(architecture)." ;
        }
        return ;
    }
    if ! $(emulator)
    {
        fail $(condition) : "no QEMU emulator is known for"
            "architecture=$(architecture) address-model=$(address-model),"
            "give it with <qemu-emulator>$(target)=qemu-... in using qemu." ;
        return ;
    }

    local program = $(.wrapper) [ program $(emulator) ] ;
    local command = $(program) [ sysroot-option $(target) : $(emulator) ]
        $(.qemu-options) ;
    if $(.debug-configuration)
    {
        ECHO "notice: [qemu] architecture=$(architecture)"
            "address-model=$(address-model): $(command:J= )" ;
    }

    # One condition for each <instruction-set> value, and one for none, so that
    # exactly one matches any test.
    local cpus ;
    for local i in "" [ instruction-sets $(architecture) ]
    {
        local cpu = [ cpu-option $(i) ] ;
        cpus += $(cpu[2]) ;
        local launcher = $(command) $(cpu) ;
        toolset.flags testing LAUNCHER $(condition)/<instruction-set>$(i)
            : "$(launcher:J= )" : unchecked ;
    }
    inspect $(program) : $(emulator) : $(.version) : $(cpus) ;
}


# Sets the launchers of the tests for bare metal targets.
#
local rule configure-bare-metal ( )
{
    for local os in elf none
    {
        if ! $(.machines)
        {
            fail <target-os>$(os)/<testing.launcher> : "bare metal tests"
                "need a machine to run on, give it with <qemu-machine>..."
                "in using qemu." ;
        }
        else
        {
            fail <target-os>$(os)/<architecture>/<testing.launcher> :
                "bare metal tests need an architecture and address-model to"
                "choose the QEMU emulator." ;
            local address-models = [ feature.values <address-model> ] ;
            for local a in [ feature.values <architecture> ]
            {
                for local m in "" $(address-models)
                {
                    configure-bare-metal-target $(os) : $(a) : $(m) ;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}


# The shell commands that pass the test, $1, as the kernel, and it and its
# arguments, the rest, with semihosting, doubling any commas in them as QEMU
# needs.
.semihosting = "k=$1; e=`printf %s \"$1\" | sed s/,/,,/g`; shift;"
    "for x in \"$@\"; do"
    "e=\"$e,arg=`printf %s \"$x\" | sed s/,/,,/g`\"; done;" ;
.semihosting-options = "-kernel \"$k\""
    "-semihosting-config \"enable=on,target=native,arg=$e\"" ;


# Sets the launcher of the tests for one bare metal <target-os>,
# <architecture> and <address-model>. The test is loaded as the kernel of the
# machine, and given its arguments, and run with semihosting.
#
local rule configure-bare-metal-target ( os : architecture : address-model ? )
{
    local target = $(architecture).$(address-model) ;
    local condition = <target-os>$(os)/<architecture>$(architecture)
        /<address-model>$(address-model)/<testing.launcher> ;
    condition = $(condition:J=) ;
    local emulator = $(.system.$(target)) ;

    if ! $(address-model)
    {
        fail $(condition) : "bare metal tests need an address-model to choose"
            "the QEMU emulator." ;
        return ;
    }
    if ! $(emulator)
    {
        fail $(condition) : "no QEMU system emulator is known for"
            "architecture=$(architecture) address-model=$(address-model),"
            "give it with <qemu-system>$(target)=qemu-system-... in using"
            "qemu." ;
        return ;
    }

    local program = [ program $(emulator) : host ] ;
    local cpus ;
    for local i in "" [ instruction-sets $(architecture) ]
    {
        local machine = $(.machine.$(i)) $(.machine.$(target)) $(.machine) ;
        if ! $(machine)
        {
            fail $(condition)/<instruction-set>$(i) : "no machine to run"
                "architecture=$(architecture) address-model=$(address-model)"
                "instruction-set=$(i) on, give it with <qemu-machine>... in"
                "using qemu." ;
        }
        else
        {
            local cpu = [ cpu-option $(i) ] ;
            cpus += $(cpu[2]) ;
            local qemu = exec $(program) -M $(machine[1]) $(cpu) -display none
                $(.system-options) $(.qemu-options) $(.semihosting-options) ;
            toolset.flags testing LAUNCHER $(condition)/<instruction-set>$(i)
                : "sh -c '$(.semihosting:J= ) $(qemu:J= )' qemu"
                : unchecked ;
        }
    }
    inspect $(program) : $(emulator) : $(.system-version) : $(cpus) ;
}


# Makes the tests matching the condition fail with the message.
#
local rule fail ( condition : message + )
{
    if [ os.name ] = NT
    {
        # The command shell needs < and > escaped.
        local escaped = [ regex.replace "$(message:J= )" "([<>])" "^\\1" ] ;
        toolset.flags testing LAUNCHER $(condition)
            : "cmd /c \"echo error: qemu: $(escaped) 1>&2 && exit /b 1\" &&"
            : unchecked ;
    }
    else
    {
        toolset.flags testing LAUNCHER $(condition)
            : "sh -c \"echo 'error: qemu: $(message:J= )' >&2 ; exit 1\" qemu"
            : unchecked ;
    }
}


# Returns the -L system root option for the target and QEMU program.
#
local rule sysroot-option ( target : emulator )
{
    local sysroot = $(.user-sysroot.$(target)) $(.user-sysroot) ;
    if ! $(sysroot) && $(.wrapper)
    {
        # Can not look where QEMU runs, and QEMU ignores a missing one.
        sysroot = /usr/$(.sysroot.$(emulator)[1]) ;
    }
    if ! $(sysroot) && ! [ os.environ QEMU_LD_PREFIX ]
    {
        for local t in $(.sysroot.$(emulator))
        {
            if ! $(sysroot) && [ path.exists /usr/$(t) ]
            {
                sysroot = /usr/$(t) ;
            }
        }
    }
    if $(sysroot)
    {
        return -L \"$(sysroot[1])\" ;
    }
}
